## Why This Is Asked
This question probes KCNA security design, tenant isolation, and practical key management choices under constraints like rotation and revocation.

## Key Concepts
- Envelope encryption with per-tenant DEKs
- Hierarchical KMS/HSM wrapping and rotation
- Per-tenant access checks and audit proofs
- Rollout/rollback with minimal downtime and observability

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode for envelope encryption
function encryptEvent(event, tenantId, kms, store) {
  const dek = store.getDEK(tenantId);
  const ciphertext = aesGcmEncrypt(event.payload, dek);
  return { ciphertext, headers: { tenantId, keyId: dek.id, epoch: dek.epoch } };
}
function decryptEvent(record, store) {
  const dek = store.getDEK(record.headers.tenantId, record.headers.keyId);
  return aesGcmDecrypt(record.ciphertext, dek);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate rotation can be performed with zero downtime?
- How would you handle compromised KEKs and re-encryption scope?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests knowledge of per-tenant security, ACLs, and token lifecycles in KCNA. Assesses gateway/broker enforcement, rotation and revocation strategies, and robust auditing under multi-tenant pressure. Calls for practical test plans and rollback considerations.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant ACLs with topic-level granularity
- JWT/OAuth tokens and short-lived credentials
- Gateway and broker ACL enforcement and performance impact
- Rotation, revocation, and audit logging
- Observability and test strategies with edge cases

## Code Example

```javascript
// Implementation snippet: token + ACL check
function authorize(token, topic, operation) {
  const claims = decodeJWT(token);
  if (Date.now() >= claims.exp) return false;
  if (!claims.tenants.includes(claims.tenant)) return false;
  const acl = ACL_STORE[claims.tenant]?.[topic];
  if (!acl) return false;
  return acl.includes(operation);
}
```

