- How does this scale to many keys and high throughput?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests real-time handling of dynamic policies, data privacy, and end-to-end guarantees in KCNA.

## Key Concepts
- Dynamic per-tenant policy loading from a KCNA topic
- Real-time cache updates with atomic policy changes
- Data redaction strategies (none, partial, full)
- Idempotent sinks and at-least-once guarantees
- Testing under policy updates and message reordering

## Code Example
```javascript
function redact(customer, level) {
  if (level === 'full') return { name: null, email: null, card: null };
  if (level === 'partial') {
    const maskedCard = customer.card.replace(/\d(?=\d{4})/g, '*');
    const maskedEmail = customer.email.replace(/([^@]{1,2})[^@]*(@.*)/, (m, p1, p2) => p1[0] + '**' + p2);
    return { ...customer, card: maskedCard, email: maskedEmail };
  }
  return customer;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Leverages real-world needs for privacy-preserving analytics in a multi-tenant KCNA deployment, including DP budgeting, auditability, and safe exposure of aggregates.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant isolation via topic prefixes and ACLs
- Differential privacy budgeting and noise mechanisms
- Streaming topology with processors and backpressure
- Tamper-resistance via cryptographic proofs (hash chains, Merkle) and audit logs
- Canary rollout and rollback strategies

## Code Example

```javascript
function addLaplaceNoise(value, epsilon, sensitivity){
  const rnd = Math.random() - 0.5;
  const scale = sensitivity / epsilon;
  // simple Laplace sample using inverse CDF
  const noise = -scale * Math.sign(rnd) * Math.log(1 - 2 * Math.abs(rnd));
  return value + noise;
}
```

